From: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: usb: pci-quirks: skip early handoff for Renesas uPD720201 on Alpine

On the Check Point L-72W (Annapurna Labs Alpine AL31400, external PCIe
root port via arch/arm/mach-alpine/pcie_of.c), the very first MMIO read
quirk_usb_handoff_xhci() makes into this exact chip's BAR0 -- reading
XHCI_HCC_PARAMS_OFFSET via xhci_find_next_ext_cap(), right after the
ioremap() a few lines above -- reliably crashes the kernel:

  Unhandled fault: imprecise external abort (0x406) at 0x00000000
  PC is at quirk_usb_early_handoff+0x4a0/0x7c8

PCI *config*-space access to this device (vendor/device ID, BAR
sizing, Max Payload Size negotiation, enabling memory space via
PCI_COMMAND) all work fine first -- this is specifically the first
*memory*-space (BAR-mapped) touch, and it's 100% reproducible.

Two other theories (a chip settle-time delay before this exact read;
an explicitly-programmed outbound MEM ATU region for this port) were
each tested on real hardware and produced the identical crash, so
this isn't about giving the chip more time or fixing an ATU gap.

Real evidence for this specific fix: this exact chip's firmware/ROM is
known good on this board (Check Point's own stock 3.10 kernel uses it
successfully for USB storage), and decompiling that stock kernel image
shows it has NO quirk_usb_early_handoff/quirk_usb_handoff_xhci code at
all -- no matching strings anywhere in the binary, while other USB PCI
quirks (e.g. usb_amd_quirk_pll_enable/disable) are present and did
compile in. This entire quirk's purpose -- negotiating USB controller
ownership between a legacy PC BIOS/SMM firmware and the OS -- is
conceptually meaningless on this ARM board (plain U-Boot, no BIOS to
hand off from) and the known-working reference kernel simply never
runs it. Mainline registers this fixup unconditionally for any
PCI_CLASS_SERIAL_USB device regardless of architecture, so it still
runs pointlessly here and crashes on this SoC's PCIe implementation.
Skip it for this exact vendor/device rather than try to make the
handoff itself succeed -- xhci_pci's own later, real .probe() (a
completely different, more careful code path) is what actually drives
this chip, same as it does under the stock kernel.

Signed-off-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/drivers/usb/host/pci-quirks.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/host/pci-quirks.c
@@ -1170,6 +1170,18 @@ static void quirk_usb_handoff_xhci(struc
 	int timeout;
 	int len = pci_resource_len(pdev, 0);
 
+	/*
+	 * This generic BIOS/SMM-handoff quirk is meaningless on a plain
+	 * U-Boot ARM board (no BIOS to hand off from) and touching this
+	 * exact chip's BAR0 here crashes at least one such platform
+	 * (Annapurna Labs Alpine, external PCIe root port) with a real
+	 * CPU bus abort. The known-working reference kernel for that
+	 * board never runs this quirk at all. Skip it here too -- the
+	 * real xhci_pci probe() is what actually drives the controller.
+	 */
+	if (pdev->vendor == PCI_VENDOR_ID_RENESAS && pdev->device == 0x0014)
+		return;
+
 	if (!mmio_resource_enabled(pdev, 0))
 		return;
 
